<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Front Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1782–1885</span> · Also known as Wang Na</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Front Palace, or Wang Na, was the residence and office of the uparat, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> or 'second king' of the Siamese court, and by extension the office itself. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> it stood north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>, and its holder commanded his own officials and troops. After the Front Palace crisis of 1874–1875 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> left the post vacant on the viceroy's death in 1885 and abolished it; the palace now houses the National Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/front-palace.jpg" alt="Front Palace" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis Chit · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Vichaichan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
